// ============================================================================
// Fadesrek Academy — School Management System
// PostgreSQL schema (Prisma) — the data layer behind the RBAC catalogue and
// every module the front end already implements against an in-memory store.
// ============================================================================
//
// Conventions
// -----------
// • Primary keys        id String @id @default(uuid()) @db.Uuid — a UUID per
//   row, not a serial int, so a record created offline or merged in from
//   another environment never collides with one already in the table.
//
// • Soft deletes        deletedAt DateTime? — a row is never physically
//   removed by the application. Every query that means "still alive" filters
//   deletedAt IS NULL (Prisma middleware / a repository base class is the
//   right place to enforce that consistently — not shown here, since it's
//   application code, not schema).
//
// • Status               status RecordStatus @default(ACTIVE) — the generic
//   lifecycle flag (active / inactive / archived), independent of any
//   domain-specific workflow state a model also carries — e.g. Application
//   has both its own `stage` (the admissions pipeline) and this generic
//   `status` (is the row itself live).
//
// • Optimistic locking / versioning   version Int @default(1) — the service
//   layer increments this on every update and includes it in the WHERE
//   clause: UPDATE ... SET version = version + 1 WHERE id = $1 AND version =
//   $2. A zero-row result means someone else wrote first. Prisma has no
//   built-in optimistic-concurrency middleware, so enforcement lives in the
//   service layer — this column is what makes that enforceable, and doubles
//   as the row's revision number.
//
// • Audit trail          createdAt/updatedAt/deletedAt record *when*;
//   createdById/updatedById/deletedById record *who*, in one of two ways:
//     - Primary business records — the ~30 tables below where "who touched
//       this" carries real operational or compliance weight (people, money,
//       approvals, submissions, uploads) — carry a real foreign key to
//       User, enforced at the database level, with a matching back-relation
//       on User.
//     - Reference and catalogue tables (the academic calendar, the
//       class/subject list, salary grades, the fee schedule, CBT content,
//       the RBAC catalogue itself) still carry the three *_by columns,
//       indexed, but as plain UUID columns rather than enforced relations.
//       Modelling all ~55 tables as full relations to User would give User
//       three back-reference arrays per table — 150+ fields on one model —
//       for tables whose edit history is rarely queried by "who". The
//       column survives either way; only the enforced-relation ceremony is
//       scoped to where it earns its keep.
//
// • Junction / line-item tables (RolePermission, ClassSubject,
//   StudentGuardian, CbtQuestionOption, AttendanceRecord, InvoiceLine,
//   TransportStop, GalleryImage) intentionally skip the full audit block.
//   A line item's lifecycle is owned by its parent — nobody soft-deletes one
//   invoice line independently of the invoice it belongs to — so these
//   carry only createdAt and the foreign keys that make them a link.
//
// • AuditLog and Session are append-only / ephemeral by design and are
//   exempt from the standard block for the same reason a ledger doesn't
//   edit its own past entries: mutating an audit log defeats its purpose.
//
// • Money is stored in kobo (1/100 of a naira) as Int, never as a float —
//   the same rule the application layer already follows.
//
// • Table and multi-word column names are mapped to snake_case (@@map /
//   @map); Prisma models and fields stay PascalCase/camelCase.
// ============================================================================

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider  = "postgresql"
  url       = env("DATABASE_URL")
  // Supabase's transaction pooler (DATABASE_URL, port 6543) doesn't support
  // the session-level features (advisory locks, prepared statements)
  // Prisma Migrate needs — directUrl points migrations at the session-mode
  // pooler instead. App runtime queries always use DATABASE_URL.
  directUrl = env("DIRECT_URL")
}

